any disables type checking completely, allowing any operation which can lead to runtime errors.
unknown is a safer alternative where TypeScript forces you to narrow the type before using it. 
I prefer unknown in cases like API responses or error handling to maintain type safety.

1)When to use "any"?
Use any only when you have no choice:
✅ Valid cases:
Migrating JS → TS quickly
Third-party libraries without types
Temporary debugging

👉 Example:
let data: any = JSON.parse(response);

2)when to use "unknown"?
Use unknown when:
You don’t know the type yet
Handling API responses
Catch blocks
try {
  // code
} catch (error: unknown) {
  if (error instanceof Error) {
    console.log(error.message);
  }
}


Disadvantages
❌ any
No autocomplete safety
No error detection
Bugs appear at runtime (worst kind)
⚠️ unknown
Requires extra checks
Slightly more verbose code
